      "text": "To determine the type of curve on which all the points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie, we need to eliminate the parameter \\(t\\) and express \\(y\\) as a function of \\(x\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\]\n\nFirst, let's simplify the expressions for \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n\n1. Simplify \\(x\\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\nSo, we have:\n\\[ x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\nSolving for \\(\\frac{1}{t}\\):\n\\[ \\frac{1}{t} = x - 1 \\]\nTaking the reciprocal of both sides:\n\\[ t = \\frac{1}{x - 1} \\]\n\n2. Simplify \\(y\\):\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\nSubstitute \\( t = \\frac{1}{x - 1} \\):\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{x - 1}} = 1 - (x - 1) = 1 - x + 1 = 2 - x \\]\nSo, we have:\n\\[ y = 2 - x \\]\n\nThis is the equation of a line. Therefore, all the plotted points \\((x, y)\\) lie on a line.\n\nThe answer is: \\(\\boxed{A}\\)",
